.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESM-RENAME-BUCKET 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-esm-rename-bucket \- give a bucket another name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli esm rename-bucket
.BR \-\-bucket | \-b
.I ERN
.BR \-\-new\-name | \-n
.I NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
Gives a bucket another name.
.PP
Nothing is copied and no object moves. What changes is the name \- and
with it everything that spells the name out, because a bucket's name is
not stored in one place. It is in the bucket's own ERN, in every object's
reference to the bucket, in every object's ERN (which carries the bucket
name ahead of the key), and in every subscription watching the bucket.
All of them are rewritten together, so the bucket stays reachable from
its contents and its contents stay reachable from it.
.PP
The answer reports how many objects and subscriptions were rewritten, so
a rename of a large bucket says what it did rather than only that it
finished.
.PP
The bucket is addressed by ERN rather than by its current name, so the
request cannot be ambiguous about which bucket it means.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fIERN\fR
Euclid resource name of the bucket to rename. Required.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-new\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Name the bucket should have. Required.
.PP
The bucket may be given by
.B name
instead of by ERN, in which case it is resolved in your session's account
and namespace; see
.B NAMES AND ERNS
in
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1).
.SH WHAT IS REFUSED
.TP
.B A name that is taken
Two buckets cannot share a name. An operator who meant to merge two
buckets wants
.BR euclid-cli-esm-copy-object (1)
or
.BR euclid-cli-esm-move-object (1)
instead.
.TP
.B A bucket a transfer server is serving
An FTP or SFTP server names the bucket it stores uploads in, and renaming
that bucket would leave it pointing at an ERN nothing answers to \- with
clients mid-session. The rename is refused and names the server; stop it,
or point it at another bucket with
.BR euclid-cli-ets-update-server (1),
and rename afterwards.
.PP
References outside ESM are deliberately not rewritten from here. A
transfer server's definition belongs to
.BR euclid-cli-ets (1),
and editing another module's records behind its back would leave it
holding a definition it never agreed to.
.SH EVENTS
A rename publishes
.I esm.bucket.modified
carrying both the new and the previous name and ERN, so a listener that
keeps its own index can follow the bucket rather than lose sight of it.
.PP
No object event is published: the objects did not change, only what they
are called. A listener that stores object ERNs should treat the bucket
event as its cue to re-read them \- see
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The bucket was renamed. Its new name and ERN are printed as JSON, with
the number of objects and subscriptions rewritten.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, no session, the bucket does not exist or
belongs to another account, the new name is already taken or is the
bucket's current name, or a transfer server is serving the bucket.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm rename-bucket \e
    --bucket ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:bucket:reports \e
    --new-name archive
.fi
.RE
.PP
prints
.PP
.RS
.nf
{"name":"archive",
 "ern":"ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:bucket:archive",
 "objects":2,"subscriptions":1}
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-create-bucket (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-delete-bucket (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-rename-object (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-update-server (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1)
